Description
Publishing a dataset release against the deployed corpus causes the backend process to terminate.

POST /api/v1/admin/dataset-releases returns 502 Bad Gateway.

Azure App Service logs show:

RangeError: Invalid string length
    at JSON.stringify (<anonymous>)
    at Object.createRelease (.../dataset-release.service.js:43:35)
The release service currently materialises the complete release artifact and serialises it using a single JSON.stringify(...).

The current corpus contains approximately 3.2 million deliveries, making the canonical JSON artifact too large to safely represent as one JavaScript string.

Expected behaviour
Dataset releases should be publishable at the representative production corpus size without requiring the complete artifact to exist in application memory.

Release generation must preserve:

deterministic ordering;
immutable versioned snapshots;
field/schema documentation;
SHA-256 checksum reproducibility;
event count;
stable public artifact retrieval.
Acceptance Criteria
Release events are read from PostgreSQL incrementally using a cursor or deterministic keyset pagination.
The JSON artifact is produced incrementally rather than by serialising the complete dataset with one JSON.stringify.
SHA-256 is calculated incrementally over the exact artifact bytes.
The completed release artifact is persisted through the approved object-storage boundary.
PostgreSQL retains the immutable release metadata and artifact reference rather than requiring the whole artifact in application memory.
Public artifact retrieval streams the stored release artifact.
Release generation works against representative multi-million-event data.
Failed release generation does not terminate the backend process.
Partial artifacts are cleaned up when generation fails.
Existing version-reuse and release immutability behaviour remains intact.
API, database and object-storage tests cover successful and interrupted publication.
Documentation is updated to describe the storage and generation lifecycle.
